1
00:00:02,182 --> 00:00:05,168
Now let's dive right in and let's start with

2
00:00:05,473 --> 00:00:08,266
promises. So here we're in the JavaScript

3
00:00:08,298 --> 00:00:11,845
refresher part now, and promises are a core

4
00:00:12,134 --> 00:00:14,365
JavaScript feature, part of modern

5
00:00:14,461 --> 00:00:17,415
JavaScript, natively supported by modern

6
00:00:17,511 --> 00:00:18,394
JavaScript.

7
00:00:18,972 --> 00:00:21,588
And a promise is in the end just an object

8
00:00:22,021 --> 00:00:25,986
that wraps the execution of asynchronous or

9
00:00:26,291 --> 00:00:28,072
synchronous code, you could say.

10
00:00:28,922 --> 00:00:31,614
It's an object that's tied to a function

11
00:00:31,630 --> 00:00:34,242
which executes code, asynchronous or

12
00:00:34,306 --> 00:00:37,062
synchronous code, so code that finishes

13
00:00:37,319 --> 00:00:39,706
immediately or that takes a bit longer, so

14
00:00:39,738 --> 00:00:40,123
to say.

15
00:00:41,004 --> 00:00:43,312
And a promise then is able to basically

16
00:00:43,825 --> 00:00:47,318
react to 2 results of that code execution:

17
00:00:48,055 --> 00:00:50,523
that the execution completed successfully.

18
00:00:50,955 --> 00:00:53,968
In that case, you can schedule some code to

19
00:00:54,000 --> 00:00:57,338
be executed once the promise resolved

20
00:00:57,386 --> 00:00:58,221
successfully

21
00:00:58,896 --> 00:01:01,803
by calling the then method on the promise

22
00:01:02,252 --> 00:01:05,481
object and passing a callback to then that

23
00:01:05,481 --> 00:01:08,420
should execute once that wrapped operation

24
00:01:08,452 --> 00:01:08,870
finished,

25
00:01:09,721 --> 00:01:12,821
or that wrapped operation did not finish

26
00:01:12,869 --> 00:01:15,968
successfully, so it completed with an error.

27
00:01:16,627 --> 00:01:18,892
In that case, the promise gives you another

28
00:01:18,892 --> 00:01:20,723
method where you can pass a callback

29
00:01:20,739 --> 00:01:22,553
function to, the catch method,

30
00:01:23,147 --> 00:01:25,458
and that allows you to execute code if the

31
00:01:25,538 --> 00:01:27,528
operation did not complete.

32
00:01:28,700 --> 00:01:32,263
Now, both then and catch, so both these

33
00:01:32,375 --> 00:01:34,992
methods in the end automatically always

34
00:01:35,008 --> 00:01:36,645
return a new promise

35
00:01:37,174 --> 00:01:40,063
on which you again can listen with then and

36
00:01:40,160 --> 00:01:42,439
catch to success or failure.

37
00:01:43,594 --> 00:01:45,841
That might be very abstract, so let's have a

38
00:01:45,841 --> 00:01:46,949
look at it in code.

39
00:01:47,782 --> 00:01:50,362
Now to see this in code, I prepared a very

40
00:01:50,362 --> 00:01:53,246
simple starting project which is not a Deno

41
00:01:53,471 --> 00:01:56,050
project at all. That is just regular

42
00:01:56,114 --> 00:01:58,486
browser-side JavaScript, but what you learn

43
00:01:58,502 --> 00:02:00,729
here will apply exactly in the same way to

44
00:02:00,745 --> 00:02:01,018
Deno.

45
00:02:02,027 --> 00:02:04,062
And you find this starting project attached.

46
00:02:04,511 --> 00:02:07,507
It's a simple HTML skeleton where I import

47
00:02:07,507 --> 00:02:09,382
the app.js file, which is an empty

48
00:02:09,478 --> 00:02:11,593
JavaScript file. That's all we have here.

49
00:02:12,683 --> 00:02:14,269
Now we can simply open this in

50
00:02:15,010 --> 00:02:15,627
the browser

51
00:02:16,796 --> 00:02:19,035
by double-clicking that index.html file,

52
00:02:20,139 --> 00:02:21,648
and this will then give you a blank page.

53
00:02:22,370 --> 00:02:24,064
Now to get started with promises,

54
00:02:24,645 --> 00:02:27,244
we'll use an API made available by the

55
00:02:27,244 --> 00:02:27,857
browser

56
00:02:28,599 --> 00:02:31,650
which allows us to send an HTTP request, and

57
00:02:31,650 --> 00:02:32,712
we'll use jsonplaceholder.typeicode.com,

58
00:02:35,870 --> 00:02:36,760
which gives us a couple of

59
00:02:37,343 --> 00:02:40,081
endpoints, a couple of URLs we can send such

60
00:02:40,129 --> 00:02:40,745
a request to.

61
00:02:41,585 --> 00:02:43,320
And actually, this here is pretty close to

62
00:02:43,336 --> 00:02:44,348
the code we're going to write.

63
00:02:45,280 --> 00:02:47,850
So let's grab this URL. That will be the

64
00:02:47,995 --> 00:02:50,388
dummy URL to which we send the request.

65
00:02:51,127 --> 00:02:53,858
And indeed, I will use this fetch method,

66
00:02:54,260 --> 00:02:57,071
which is available globally in the browser.

67
00:02:57,714 --> 00:03:00,686
Fetch is a method that sends an HTTP

68
00:03:00,702 --> 00:03:01,200
request.

69
00:03:02,003 --> 00:03:04,381
Now it needs the URL to which the request

70
00:03:04,381 --> 00:03:05,136
should be sent,

71
00:03:05,682 --> 00:03:07,289
and then it will give us access to the

72
00:03:07,289 --> 00:03:10,388
response. But as you can imagine, sending

73
00:03:10,404 --> 00:03:13,294
such an HTTP request simply can take a bit

74
00:03:13,375 --> 00:03:13,776
longer.

75
00:03:14,659 --> 00:03:16,698
It needs to send the request to the server,

76
00:03:17,084 --> 00:03:19,637
the server needs to prepare the response and

77
00:03:19,653 --> 00:03:20,905
send back the response.

78
00:03:21,563 --> 00:03:24,261
Therefore, fetch actually executes some

79
00:03:24,534 --> 00:03:26,766
asynchronous operation behind the scenes

80
00:03:27,504 --> 00:03:31,229
and it leverages promises to allow us as a

81
00:03:31,278 --> 00:03:34,207
developer to listen to the completion or the

82
00:03:34,207 --> 00:03:36,349
failure of those operations.

83
00:03:38,670 --> 00:03:41,994
Fetch indeed returns a promise object. So

84
00:03:42,010 --> 00:03:44,819
fetch gives us such a promise object.

85
00:03:45,464 --> 00:03:47,772
And as I mentioned on the slide, we can call

86
00:03:47,950 --> 00:03:49,396
then on the promise.

87
00:03:50,296 --> 00:03:52,337
And I just placed it in a new line here.

88
00:03:52,562 --> 00:03:54,394
It's the same as if I would have placed it

89
00:03:54,394 --> 00:03:57,496
here. I just placed it in a new line for

90
00:03:57,496 --> 00:03:58,252
better readability.

91
00:03:59,530 --> 00:04:02,106
Then, then takes a function as an argument.

92
00:04:02,512 --> 00:04:04,631
In this case, I'm passing in an anonymous

93
00:04:04,824 --> 00:04:07,296
arrow function, which is a regular function

94
00:04:07,344 --> 00:04:08,259
in JavaScript in the end.

95
00:04:09,254 --> 00:04:12,224
And this function will be executed by this

96
00:04:12,224 --> 00:04:12,722
promise

97
00:04:13,252 --> 00:04:15,820
when the wrapped operation, which in this

98
00:04:15,820 --> 00:04:18,309
case is the sending of the HTTP request,

99
00:04:18,549 --> 00:04:21,214
completed, when it completed successfully to

100
00:04:21,214 --> 00:04:21,840
be precise.

101
00:04:22,418 --> 00:04:24,778
Here we then get an argument and that will

102
00:04:24,826 --> 00:04:26,929
be the data the promise resolved to.

103
00:04:27,475 --> 00:04:29,209
So in this case, that will be the response.

104
00:04:29,402 --> 00:04:31,392
The promise will automatically pass the

105
00:04:31,408 --> 00:04:34,138
response it received as an argument to this

106
00:04:34,138 --> 00:04:35,695
function which we passed to then.

107
00:04:36,530 --> 00:04:38,055
And in here we can then utilize the

108
00:04:38,055 --> 00:04:38,601
response.

109
00:04:39,677 --> 00:04:41,411
Now we can, for example,

110
00:04:42,807 --> 00:04:43,289
get the

111
00:04:44,172 --> 00:04:47,560
response status code, which indicates

112
00:04:47,608 --> 00:04:49,294
whether the server sent us back

113
00:04:50,434 --> 00:04:52,649
useful data or if something went wrong on

114
00:04:52,681 --> 00:04:54,094
the server. So we can

115
00:04:54,672 --> 00:04:57,032
analyze that with help of the status code.

116
00:04:57,177 --> 00:04:58,397
And here I'm just logging it.

117
00:04:59,930 --> 00:05:02,791
Now it could also fail, for example, if we

118
00:05:02,791 --> 00:05:04,344
have no network connection.

119
00:05:05,242 --> 00:05:07,846
And for that, we can add a catch method here

120
00:05:08,039 --> 00:05:08,730
after then,

121
00:05:09,502 --> 00:05:11,415
and the function we pass to catch will

122
00:05:11,495 --> 00:05:14,376
execute if any of the previous promises

123
00:05:15,119 --> 00:05:15,394
fail.

124
00:05:16,186 --> 00:05:18,093
And here we then get the error with which

125
00:05:18,206 --> 00:05:20,824
they failed. We can console.log this

126
00:05:21,583 --> 00:05:21,907
as well.

127
00:05:23,862 --> 00:05:26,373
Now what's important is is that both then

128
00:05:26,629 --> 00:05:27,175
and catch

129
00:05:27,752 --> 00:05:31,137
return new promises themselves. That's why

130
00:05:31,137 --> 00:05:33,191
we can call catch after then.

131
00:05:34,009 --> 00:05:36,720
So just like fetch returns a promise, then

132
00:05:36,912 --> 00:05:39,239
and catch then also always return new

133
00:05:39,239 --> 00:05:42,062
promises. You of course then don't have to

134
00:05:42,190 --> 00:05:44,821
listen to those promises with then, but you

135
00:05:44,853 --> 00:05:45,222
can.

136
00:05:46,137 --> 00:05:48,270
So even if you don't return anything in

137
00:05:48,286 --> 00:05:50,019
those anonymous functions on your own,

138
00:05:50,548 --> 00:05:54,044
then and catch always give you new promises,

139
00:05:55,103 --> 00:05:57,652
which then of course don't do anything

140
00:05:57,828 --> 00:06:00,153
asynchronous necessarily, which don't

141
00:06:00,169 --> 00:06:02,542
necessarily send a new request, but which

142
00:06:02,574 --> 00:06:04,306
simply resolve instantly.

143
00:06:05,621 --> 00:06:07,208
But it is a promise object.

144
00:06:08,507 --> 00:06:10,014
So in this case, if we save that,

145
00:06:10,591 --> 00:06:12,579
we go back to our page, and I open up the

146
00:06:12,579 --> 00:06:14,760
developer tools and the JavaScript console

147
00:06:14,760 --> 00:06:15,770
there, and I reload,

148
00:06:16,427 --> 00:06:19,217
we see 200 in this case, which is the status

149
00:06:19,329 --> 00:06:22,310
code sent back with the response and 200

150
00:06:22,439 --> 00:06:25,018
indicates that the request was successful.

151
00:06:26,204 --> 00:06:28,287
If I, on the other hand, maybe mess up this

152
00:06:28,287 --> 00:06:29,857
URL here and save this

153
00:06:30,770 --> 00:06:33,558
and I reload, now we are in this error case.

154
00:06:34,295 --> 00:06:36,875
I'm printing the error description here. So

155
00:06:36,891 --> 00:06:38,413
now we're in the catch block

156
00:06:39,359 --> 00:06:42,083
and I will revert this now. That is how

157
00:06:42,227 --> 00:06:43,204
promises work.

158
00:06:44,166 --> 00:06:46,777
Now, instead of promises, you can also

159
00:06:46,954 --> 00:06:49,086
return something For example, here,

160
00:06:49,679 --> 00:06:51,428
the parsed response body.

161
00:06:52,005 --> 00:06:54,074
You do parse it by calling JSON

162
00:06:54,732 --> 00:06:56,801
and that actually returns a new promise.

163
00:06:57,379 --> 00:06:59,881
Now, I said that then also always returns a

164
00:06:59,881 --> 00:07:02,015
new promise and if you then return a new

165
00:07:02,127 --> 00:07:04,260
promise inside of that anonymous function,

166
00:07:04,838 --> 00:07:07,661
that default promise, which would always be

167
00:07:07,661 --> 00:07:10,500
returned, will be replaced by that promise

168
00:07:10,533 --> 00:07:12,506
you manually returned in there.

169
00:07:13,276 --> 00:07:15,233
And then we can add another then block here

170
00:07:15,682 --> 00:07:18,941
to get the extracted data in this case

171
00:07:19,776 --> 00:07:22,200
and console.log that maybe.

172
00:07:23,468 --> 00:07:25,941
And that here is a concept which is also

173
00:07:25,941 --> 00:07:28,574
sometimes called promise chaining because

174
00:07:28,606 --> 00:07:31,512
we're chaining handler after handler.

175
00:07:32,202 --> 00:07:34,787
Here we first of all react to the response,

176
00:07:35,108 --> 00:07:37,596
then we react to the data extraction. And

177
00:07:37,596 --> 00:07:40,550
this catch here, this error function, will

178
00:07:40,550 --> 00:07:43,633
execute whenever any of these 3 prior

179
00:07:43,777 --> 00:07:44,789
promises fails.

180
00:07:45,729 --> 00:07:47,210
So if I now save that and reload,

181
00:07:47,838 --> 00:07:49,706
we see the extracted data here.

182
00:07:50,882 --> 00:07:53,394
Now that's a promise in a nutshell, and it

183
00:07:53,394 --> 00:07:56,519
really is a core JavaScript feature baked

184
00:07:56,600 --> 00:07:57,921
into JavaScript.

185
00:07:58,629 --> 00:08:01,190
Now besides those built-in promises like

186
00:08:01,207 --> 00:08:04,201
fetch, you can also build your own promises.

187
00:08:06,021 --> 00:08:07,816
So let's build our own promise now,

188
00:08:08,673 --> 00:08:11,115
and for that I'll have my own promise here

189
00:08:11,752 --> 00:08:13,966
by calling new Promise.

190
00:08:14,720 --> 00:08:17,737
JavaScript comes with this Promise class,

191
00:08:17,929 --> 00:08:19,902
with this Promise constructor function,

192
00:08:20,496 --> 00:08:22,373
and we can instantiate it like this to get a

193
00:08:22,421 --> 00:08:23,464
Promise object.

194
00:08:24,186 --> 00:08:26,993
Now I mentioned that such a Promise object

195
00:08:27,218 --> 00:08:29,705
needs to wrap some code that should be

196
00:08:29,705 --> 00:08:32,641
executed, and that can be synchronous or

197
00:08:32,913 --> 00:08:35,095
asynchronous code. So it can be code that

198
00:08:35,095 --> 00:08:36,860
takes a bit longer or that finishes

199
00:08:36,860 --> 00:08:39,568
immediately. To tell the promise which code

200
00:08:39,568 --> 00:08:42,661
it should execute, we pass a function to the

201
00:08:42,773 --> 00:08:43,077
promise.

202
00:08:43,734 --> 00:08:45,753
And in that function, we have the code that

203
00:08:45,753 --> 00:08:47,804
should be wrapped by the promise.

204
00:08:48,380 --> 00:08:51,136
Here, for example, I could set a timeout

205
00:08:51,761 --> 00:08:53,668
with the built-in setTimeout function,

206
00:08:54,485 --> 00:08:56,600
and that takes a callback function, then

207
00:08:57,609 --> 00:08:59,291
this function here as a first argument,

208
00:08:59,692 --> 00:09:01,358
which should execute once the timer

209
00:09:01,358 --> 00:09:01,903
completed.

210
00:09:02,912 --> 00:09:06,213
Now we just need to let the wrapping promise

211
00:09:06,325 --> 00:09:06,565
know

212
00:09:07,126 --> 00:09:09,788
once our wrapped operation is done.

213
00:09:10,510 --> 00:09:13,140
And for that, to this function which we pass

214
00:09:13,140 --> 00:09:14,407
to the promise constructor,

215
00:09:14,984 --> 00:09:18,464
we can pass 2 parameters, and the values for

216
00:09:18,512 --> 00:09:19,346
those parameters

217
00:09:19,875 --> 00:09:22,265
will be provided by the promise,

218
00:09:23,018 --> 00:09:25,071
and that's the resolve and the reject

219
00:09:25,424 --> 00:09:25,873
function.

220
00:09:26,482 --> 00:09:28,792
So we get 2 parameters here,

221
00:09:29,337 --> 00:09:31,069
each of them will get a

222
00:09:31,662 --> 00:09:34,342
function argument by the promise

223
00:09:34,550 --> 00:09:35,177
automatically,

224
00:09:35,771 --> 00:09:37,745
and therefore we can call each of those

225
00:09:37,938 --> 00:09:39,190
inside of the promise.

226
00:09:39,880 --> 00:09:41,887
So here we can call resolve inside of the

227
00:09:41,887 --> 00:09:44,745
setTimeout function and then resolve to a

228
00:09:44,761 --> 00:09:47,891
value of our choice, to a number or for

229
00:09:47,891 --> 00:09:48,983
example to a string,

230
00:09:49,577 --> 00:09:51,150
timer completed.

231
00:09:52,611 --> 00:09:54,923
If we would call reject instead,

232
00:09:55,758 --> 00:09:57,636
we would basically throw an error

233
00:09:58,246 --> 00:10:01,264
and any catch blocks on that promise would

234
00:10:01,296 --> 00:10:01,714
trigger.

235
00:10:02,324 --> 00:10:04,266
But I don't wanna reject here, I wanna

236
00:10:04,266 --> 00:10:04,635
resolve.

237
00:10:05,534 --> 00:10:08,407
And the built-in promise returned by fetch

238
00:10:08,921 --> 00:10:11,313
basically just has code like this inside of

239
00:10:11,313 --> 00:10:15,020
it for sending an HTTP request. And then it

240
00:10:15,020 --> 00:10:17,509
basically just has an if check inside of it

241
00:10:17,509 --> 00:10:19,403
where it checks whether everything

242
00:10:20,061 --> 00:10:22,308
worked, in which case it resolves with the

243
00:10:22,308 --> 00:10:25,454
response, or whether an error occurred, in

244
00:10:25,518 --> 00:10:27,621
which case it rejects with that error.

245
00:10:27,782 --> 00:10:29,758
That's how you can think about that built-in

246
00:10:29,855 --> 00:10:30,272
promise.

247
00:10:31,140 --> 00:10:33,550
Here we have our own promise, and on that

248
00:10:33,550 --> 00:10:36,700
promise we can now again use then to react

249
00:10:36,700 --> 00:10:38,708
to the completion of the wrapped operation.

250
00:10:39,576 --> 00:10:41,970
We pass a function to it, and here as an

251
00:10:41,986 --> 00:10:44,863
argument we get the data with which our

252
00:10:44,895 --> 00:10:45,907
promise resolves.

253
00:10:46,598 --> 00:10:49,539
So in this case, for our own promise, we get

254
00:10:49,539 --> 00:10:50,808
that string eventually.

255
00:10:51,467 --> 00:10:53,508
So here I can then console.log

256
00:10:54,310 --> 00:10:54,936
myData.

257
00:10:55,978 --> 00:10:58,610
And if we save that and reload, we don't

258
00:10:58,610 --> 00:11:01,754
just get the HTTP response but also timer

259
00:11:01,754 --> 00:11:03,535
completed after 1 second.

260
00:11:04,707 --> 00:11:06,808
Now of course that's just a dummy promise.

261
00:11:07,033 --> 00:11:09,905
This isn't any useful code, but this shows

262
00:11:09,937 --> 00:11:12,520
you what promises are and how they work.

263
00:11:13,066 --> 00:11:15,376
And we'll see promises a lot throughout this

264
00:11:15,376 --> 00:11:17,061
course. Therefore, you really need to

265
00:11:17,061 --> 00:11:18,521
understand this feature.
